    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"The original context from which these figures were disinterred is unknown. Their similarities in size, color, and decoration, however, suggest that they were found together and were made as a pair. The male displays an intricately decorated textile slung over the left side of his chest, an indication of high rank. A musician, he strikes a turtle carapace with a deer antler. The female wears a ﬁne skirt and has scarified shoulders. Such male-female pairs in tombs have been interpreted as marital couples, although the relationship of this pair to the person or persons with whom they were interred remains difficult to know. Are they portraits of lineage founders? Are they portraits of living descendants of the interred, perpetually present to care for their ancestors? Might they allude to a moment in the biography of the deceased?\n",
            "remarks":"AAA1_20-T6-3_CLA_FA_9_17_25.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
